WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.267
The early field and lab evidence: large gains, unevenly spread.

2
00:00:04.547 --> 00:00:07.533
Customer support (Brynjolfsson, Li, and Raymond).

3
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:24.539
First released as an NBER working paper in 2023 and published in the Quarterly Journal of Economics in 2025, this study followed the staggered rollout of an AI assistant to over 5,000 customer support agents.

4
00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:38.963
Access raised productivity, measured as issues resolved per hour, by about 14% on average, with gains of about 34% for novice and lower-skilled workers and minimal effect for the most experienced.

5
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:43.360
The authors also report improved customer sentiment and retention.

6
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:49.464
The interpretation they offer: the tool spread the tacit knowledge of top performers to everyone else.

7
00:00:49.744 --> 00:00:53.520
GitHub Copilot (Peng and colleagues, 2023).

8
00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:04.104
In a controlled experiment, developers asked to implement an HTTP server in JavaScript finished about 56% faster with Copilot than without.

9
00:01:04.384 --> 00:01:10.400
The task was small, self-contained, and well specified, which is where code completion helps most.

10
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:16.419
These two studies set the early narrative: big gains, especially for less experienced workers.

11
00:01:16.699 --> 00:01:18.832
Both are real and both are narrow.

12
00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:27.773
Notice the populations (support agents with a scripted job; developers on a greenfield toy task) before applying the numbers to your team.
